# C256 B staging through shared memory: feasibility and next decision

**A private three-slot `cp.async` B ring is a justified next experiment.** It
targets the observed late B data without introducing another future-B register
bank or a data-dependent carry. Its address and lifetime schedule is valid
for the existing prepared weight cache. This is a CPU proposal, not an
implemented kernel or a performance result. I would prioritize it over peeling
the final K iteration when the aim is a material C256 improvement; peeling is
the smaller, lower-risk control experiment.

The evidence is the [rolling NCU analysis](../profile/window-vector-prefetch-fp8-b15-136x240-p1-v3-20261003T062752_050764Z/ANALYSIS.md):
2,416 of 4,129 long-scoreboard samples occur at the first carry consumer of a
prefetched B load. The measured ping-pong and three-register-bank alternatives
lost, and the [v29 cache hints](cache_hint_v29_results.md.draft) also lost.
The latter retained their early request but added address/control work while
leaving the late register loads and carries intact. This motivates testing a
different data path, without assuming it will win.

The proposal changes only the nine `dense32` calls in
[`window_block_vector_prefetch.cuh`](../csrc/kernel_impl/window_block_vector_prefetch.cuh).
W2, shared A layout and reads, the ascending K32 MMA sequence, half accumulators,
normalization, publication, attention, stores and seven CTA barriers stay the
same. Keep the existing SM89+ guard and C256 restriction. No TMA, async MMA,
architecture-specific tensor-memory feature or new weight preparation is needed.

Each lane currently loads two aligned `uint4` values for one K32 step:

```text
source = weight + kk*N*32 + (row/16 + tile)*512 + lane*16
tile = 0,1; lane = 0..31; kk = 0..7
```

The same source addresses can feed two 16-byte copies into
`uint4 ring[8 warps][3 stages][2 tiles][32 lanes]`. Each lane later reads only
the two values it copied. This uses 24 KiB, has no overlapping writer or
cross-lane consumer, and preserves each warp's contiguous 512-byte vector
requests. The existing launcher already aligns the prepared cache to sixteen
bytes. Ring destinations also need sixteen-byte alignment and a shared-space
address obtained with `__cvta_generic_to_shared`.

Use one committed group for each K's pair of copies. Preissue K0 and K1 in
separate groups. Before computing each K, the proposed order is:

| Current K | Issue/commit first | Wait for all except newest | Read ring slot | Then compute |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 0 | 2 | 2 groups | 0 | 16 current-K QMMAs |
| 1 | 3 | 2 groups | 1 | 16 current-K QMMAs |
| 2 | 4 | 2 groups | 2 | 16 current-K QMMAs |
| 3 | 5 | 2 groups | 0 | 16 current-K QMMAs |
| 4 | 6 | 2 groups | 1 | 16 current-K QMMAs |
| 5 | 7 | 2 groups | 2 | 16 current-K QMMAs |
| 6 | none | 1 group | 0 | 16 current-K QMMAs |
| 7 | none | 0 groups | 1 | 16 current-K QMMAs |

`cp.async.wait_group` operates on the issuing thread's committed groups and
makes its completed copies visible to that thread. Consequently this ownership
layout needs no additional CTA barrier. The wait immediates must be explicit
2/1/0 cases, not a runtime operand. Do not interleave unrelated async groups,
combine the initial K0/K1 groups, or reuse the ring before the final drain.
These requirements follow the [PTX copy/group semantics](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/#data-movement-and-conversion-instructions-cp-async-wait-group).

The [CPU proof](../profile/cp-async-b-feasibility-cpu/analysis/proof.json)
checks all nine dense roles, eight warps and thirty-two lanes: 36,864 vector
copies, or 589,824 requested bytes per CTA. All source/destination vectors
are aligned, bounded and assigned to the expected owner. K0–K7 are copied
and consumed exactly once; K8/K9 are never formed. Negative tests reject a
non-draining tail and premature slot reuse. For K≥2, the abstract schedule
places thirty-two QMMAs before the first shared B read. K0 has no overlap;
K1 has sixteen QMMAs. A second CPU review independently confirmed the schedule.
This proves the proposed ownership/control model, not a future implementation.

There are two important qualifications. First, the ring's completion wait is
a real data-availability boundary, unlike a cache hint. It may simply wait
for the same memory latency at a different instruction. Second, neither
`volatile` PTX nor the source order proves early SASS issue relative to
independent arithmetic. Use compiler memory clobbers around copies and waits
to prevent invalid shared-load motion, then inspect actual async-copy PCs,
commit/wait placement and the earliest shared read. The mechanism removes a
future global-load result from GPRs; it does not guarantee that ptxas schedules
the copy with the intended two-K lead.

Costs can be bounded before compiling:

| Property per warp/window | Rolling | Proposed three-slot ring |
|---|---:|---:|
| Dense B global vector requests | 144 | 144 async requests |
| Other B vector loads, W2 | 8 | 8 unchanged |
| Dense A shared vector loads | 288 | 288 unchanged |
| Additional B shared vector loads | 0 | 144 |
| Dense QMMA instructions | 1,152 | 1,152 unchanged |
| Explicit B carry assignments | 504 | none in this design |
| Added copy commits / waits | 0 / 0 | 72 / 72 |
| Logical shared bytes per CTA | 16,384 | 40,960 |

These are source/request counts, not a promise of identical instruction
counts or physical memory transfers. Each CTA gains 576 KiB of B writes into
shared memory and the same amount of B reads, in addition to its existing A
traffic. The initial design should use ordinary vector shared loads; preserve
lane ownership and avoid a transpose or `ldmatrix` mapping change. Contiguous
sixteen-byte lanes provide a sensible starting layout, but bank-conflict and
shared-pipe behavior still require NCU.

Only the current eight-word B fragment remains a source-level register bank.
This may lower pressure compared with rolling's current/next arrays, but the
kernel's peak may occur elsewhere and address/control state can absorb the
saving. No register count can be predicted from those arrays alone. The
observed rolling allocation is 168 registers per thread: 43,008 registers for
256 threads, already restricting it to one CTA per SM. Removing eight source
words would not by itself establish two-CTA residency.

The ring would raise the launch's shared footprint from the observed 17 KiB
to roughly 41 KiB if the existing one-KiB reservation remains. It fits within
a 64-KiB shared carveout and the 48-KiB static-allocation limit. At that carveout,
shared memory alone still permits only one such CTA. Requesting 50% through
`cudaFuncAttributePreferredSharedMemoryCarveout` selects 64 KiB on SM120,
subject to the driver's documented discretion. Record the actual configuration
and occupancy rather than assuming the request was honored. Increasing the
carveout also reduces available L1 capacity. The
[CUDA programming guide](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/03-advanced/advanced-kernel-programming.html#configuring-l1-shared-memory-balance)
and [SM120 capacity table](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/05-appendices/compute-capabilities.html#features-and-technical-specifications)
support these resource limits; the B200-specific carveout table should not be
used for this RTX device.

A two-slot alternative needs only 16 KiB extra shared memory. It must **wait
and load current B into registers before reusing that slot for K+2**, then
compute current K. Its abstract lookahead is still thirty-two QMMAs, and the
CPU proof validates it separately. It cannot start the future copy while
waiting for current B, whereas the three-slot schedule can. Both choices need
a larger-than-32-KiB carveout once the observed reservation is included.
Therefore three slots are the clearer first latency experiment; two slots
are a useful capacity/control follow-up if the first result warrants one.

Start with the cache-all `cp.async.ca` form to avoid deliberately excluding L1
from this first experiment; `.cg` would be a separate L2-only control, not a
silent substitution. PTX defines those cache distinctions, and both support
the aligned sixteen-byte copy here. Their actual SM120 cache qualifiers must
be recorded from the compiled code. [PTX copy instructions](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/#data-movement-and-conversion-instructions-cp-async)

For comparison, peeling K7 from rolling can remove redundant tail predicates
and keep its current resources/data path. The saved loop has three predicate
tests and two counter updates per iteration. That makes peeling inexpensive
to test, but it does not address the measured late-B dependency; the profile
does not show control instructions as the primary stall source. Rank the
shared ring first for a latency hypothesis, and peeled K last-step control
second for a small instruction-cost hypothesis. Neither has a speed estimate.

Before any promotion, require compiled retained async copies with the intended
lead, correct wait/drain and no spills; byte-exact outputs and mutated graph
replays; tail/misalignment cases and memcheck/racecheck; then paired timings
against rolling across the existing shapes/blocks/phase/raw contracts. A useful
candidate should next show reduced B-dependent stalls without replacing them
with larger wait/shared-pipe stalls. Its physical-I/O native comparison and
the existing 85% resource gate remain separate. No acceptance claim follows
from this feasibility proof.

Reproduce the CPU proof without CUDA:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe profile/cp-async-b-feasibility-cpu/analysis/prove.py
```

Its JSON pins the inspected rolling source, launcher, SASS, NCU dependency
summary and proof script by SHA256. No active source, compiled policy, binary
or raw profiling artifact was changed, and no build or GPU operation was run.
